1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,128
In 1866, a famous
Egyptologist had a mud brick

2
00:00:05,128 --> 00:00:08,401
cut out of a ruined city in Egypt.

3
00:00:08,851 --> 00:00:12,777
He took it, he wrote, in memory
of the Israelites' forced labour.

4
00:00:13,227 --> 00:00:16,471
A botanist in Vienna
broke it open, and found

5
00:00:16,471 --> 00:00:18,923
seeds of an Ethiopian grain: teff.

6
00:00:19,373 --> 00:00:22,765
Proof, he said, that Egypt
once farmed it. Was he right?

7
00:00:24,715 --> 00:00:28,000
We went looking for the old
legend of where teff came from.

8
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:32,333
Epics, folk tales, collections.
We could not trace a single one.

9
00:00:32,783 --> 00:00:34,768
So we followed what was written down

10
00:00:34,768 --> 00:00:36,644
instead. Starting with that brick.

11
00:00:38,594 --> 00:00:41,588
The Egyptologist was Richard Lepsius. The

12
00:00:41,588 --> 00:00:44,290
ruin was Maskhuta, in the Nile Delta.

13
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:48,687
He believed it was Ramses, a city
named in the Book of Exodus.

14
00:00:49,137 --> 00:00:52,392
The botanist was Franz
Unger. Inside the brick:

15
00:00:52,392 --> 00:00:54,747
straw, barley, small wheat, weeds.

16
00:00:55,197 --> 00:00:57,459
And a few tiny seeds he named as teff.

17
00:00:57,909 --> 00:01:01,365
He said he had seen the same in a
brick from a pyramid at Dahshur.

18
00:01:01,815 --> 00:01:05,067
Egypt grew teff for thousands
of years, he wrote, then

19
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:07,596
forgot it. Would you believe him? Comment.

20
00:01:09,546 --> 00:01:12,350
Did you know? It takes
about a hundred and fifty

21
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:14,687
teff grains to match one grain of wheat.

22
00:01:15,137 --> 00:01:17,783
It is the smallest grain
of any cereal on earth.

23
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:21,539
And that is exactly why
Unger's seeds are a problem.

24
00:01:23,489 --> 00:01:27,863
Teff has a wild cousin, a weedy
grass that grows all over Egypt.

25
00:01:28,313 --> 00:01:31,255
Its seeds look almost the
same. Later botanists

26
00:01:31,255 --> 00:01:34,009
think Unger's seeds were
probably that weed.

27
00:01:34,459 --> 00:01:38,107
And nobody can check. His seeds
are no longer there to test.

28
00:01:38,557 --> 00:01:41,272
So the pyramid teff is
a claim, not a fact.

29
00:01:41,272 --> 00:01:43,357
Then where did teff really begin?

30
00:01:45,307 --> 00:01:48,187
Its wild ancestor most likely grew in the

31
00:01:48,187 --> 00:01:50,576
highlands of Ethiopia and Eritrea.

32
00:01:51,026 --> 00:01:53,711
Near Aksum, archaeologists found charred

33
00:01:53,711 --> 00:01:56,061
teff beside wheat, barley and flax.

34
00:01:56,511 --> 00:01:59,711
Some of it dates from at least
five hundred years before Christ.

35
00:02:00,161 --> 00:02:03,657
Finding it is hard. One
archaeologist had to write

36
00:02:03,657 --> 00:02:06,454
rules just to tell teff from wild grass.

37
00:02:06,904 --> 00:02:10,659
When it was first tamed, and how
many times, nobody knows yet.

38
00:02:12,609 --> 00:02:14,941
Did you know? The name teff is popularly

39
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:16,982
linked to an old word meaning lost.

40
00:02:17,432 --> 00:02:20,803
Drop one grain, the saying
goes, and it is gone for good.

41
00:02:21,253 --> 00:02:24,333
A lovely story. Other
scholars point to Arabic

42
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:26,543
instead. Nobody can prove either.

43
00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:31,319
In 2003, a Dutch company applied for a

44
00:02:31,319 --> 00:02:33,699
patent on processing teff flour.

45
00:02:34,149 --> 00:02:38,458
Baking with teff, something Ethiopian
kitchens had done for centuries.

46
00:02:38,908 --> 00:02:43,644
In 2014, the company took a Dutch
bakery to court for using it.

47
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:46,974
In 2018, a court in The Hague threw both

48
00:02:46,974 --> 00:02:49,854
patents out. Nothing inventive, it said.

49
00:02:50,304 --> 00:02:54,059
Ethiopia's ambassador called
it great news. Save this one.

50
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:59,157
A brick with a famous
claim. A name with a lovely

51
00:02:59,157 --> 00:03:01,598
story. A patent that sounded official.

52
00:03:02,048 --> 00:03:04,608
Each one changed when
someone checked the source.

53
00:03:05,058 --> 00:03:07,452
So when a post tells you
where something came

54
00:03:07,452 --> 00:03:09,154
from, ask: who checked, and how?

55
00:03:11,104 --> 00:03:13,849
Today about six million
farmers grow teff, on

56
00:03:13,849 --> 00:03:15,861
more than three million hectares.

57
00:03:16,311 --> 00:03:20,151
The tiniest grain on earth feeds
a nation, one meal at a time.

58
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:22,873
Some of us want to own every good thing.

59
00:03:22,873 --> 00:03:24,633
Others waste what we are given.

60
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:27,751
The quiet path is
gratitude: bless the bread,

61
00:03:27,751 --> 00:03:30,182
share it, and thank the God who gives it.

62
00:03:30,632 --> 00:03:32,445
Give us this day our daily bread.

63
00:03:34,395 --> 00:03:36,486
Thank you for sitting with us over coffee.

64
00:03:36,936 --> 00:03:38,408
This is Ethiopics.
